    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-30-tiktoker-faces-police-probe-over-secret-filming-using-smart-",
      "title": "TikToker faces police probe over secret filming using 'smart glasses'",
      "summary": "The \"content maker\" published videos of encounters with young women and a female Sikh shop worker where he talked of a plot to \"breed out the white race\".",
      "body": "In late June and early July he toured Scotland in a white Ford Transit van, visiting various locations including Dunoon, Glencoe, John O'Groats, Skye, Uist, Barra and Lewis. While many of his videos feature harmless banter, others have a darker side, particularly when he encounters young women or people from ethnic minorities.In one video, the 42-year-old makes sexualised comments about the appearance of two teenagers who he spots as he drives onto a small CalMac ferry. He then puts on his Meta glasses, which contain tiny cameras, gets out of his van and engages the girls and their parents in conversation, apparently without their knowledge of being filmed. In the same video he uses a strong expletive in an unprovoked outburst directed at a fellow ferry passenger.  One of the most disturbing incidents occurs on Skye where he tells a UK-born Sikh shopkeeper of Indian heritage that there is \"forced\" diversity in Europe. He goes on to refer to the Kalergi Plan - a far right conspiracy theory about a plot to \"breed out the white race\".The woman, who was running the shop with help from her teenage daughter, politely suggests his comments are \"slightly derogatory\". When he leaves, Margerison angrily describes her in his video commentary as \"condescending\" and complains that \"they don't understand\".The woman, who was born in London and has lived in Skye for 20 years, told BBC Scotland News he initiated the conversation by asking \"what are you doing here?\" but edited this out of the published footage. She said her encounter with Margerison, who is heavily built and well over 6ft tall, was \"100% intimidating\" and that she cried when she was later told by a friend that a secretly-filmed video had been published on TikTok. \"You're made to feel very small when you see a video like this,\" she said. \"And it's sad because everyone in the local community is really nice. This was really upsetting.\"While in the Hebrides, Margerison also filmed an angry encounter with an elderly couple in a supermarket after they complained that he had \"loudly and rudely\" asked them to move out of his way.He follows them around the store, at one point blocking their path, swears in front of them, accuses them of being \"mental\".He also tells them he intends to post footage of them on TikTok which he says is \"well within my rights\".Image gallerySkip image galleryImage source, YoutubeImage caption, Margerison filmed staff on the bridge of a CalMac ferry, which he had been allowed to visit as a courtesySlide 1 of 3, The bridge of a ferry including a member of staff who is being filmed from behind., Margerison filmed staff on the bridge of a CalMac ferry, which he had been allowed to visit as a courtesyBBC News has reported on cases where young women were approached and secretly recorded by men who then posted videos online and demanded money to remove them.Secret recording has also taken place by individuals - including Margerison - at various Comic Con cosplay events, prompting attendees to complain of invasion of privacy, harassment and \"body shaming\". Last week Monopoly Events, the promoter behind some of the biggest comic and entertainment conventions in the UK, banned Meta glasses from its gatherings.Meta, which owns Instagram, told BBC Scotland News it was \"actively investigating\" Margerison's accounts and would act on anything that violated its policies.On the smart glasses themselves, a spokesperson said: \"People use our glasses because they're genuinely helpful – from listening to music, to live translation or hands-free calls. \"For those wearing them and the people around them, trust matters. That's why we built privacy into our AI glasses from the ground up.\"The head of Instagram Adam Mosseri recently admitted that some users had tried to \"drill off\" the flashing light, but he claimed the latest updates meant doing so would render the glasses inoperable.Instagram has now shut down all of Margerison's accounts.TikTok said it did not permit content that harasses or bullies others, and said it took action against accounts that violated its policies. , external YouTube said it would investigate Margerison's videos on its platform. Police Scotland is understood to be investigating complaints about both Margerison's secret filming and his behaviour, and anyone who wishes to report an incident should do so using the 101 number or online. , external",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-30T22:11:12.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/ac18/live/43748fd0-8c54-11f1-ba3e-21e9a16dd8e9.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c4g369y5k8ko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-30-xbox-ceo-lays-out-priorities-in-memo-after-major-8216-reset-",
      "title": "Xbox CEO lays out priorities in memo after major &#8216;reset&#8217;",
      "summary": "Asha Sharma. | Image: The Verge, Microsoft\t\n\nAfter a massive Xbox \"reset\" that laid off thousands of employees and spun off four studios, Xbox CEO Asha Sharma wants to get Xbox back to growth. In a memo obtained by The Verge, Sharma told staff by the end of fiscal year 2027 (which runs through next June), \"we will return XBOX to player and revenue growth.\" Xbox will also \"improve profits back in l",
      "body": "After a massive Xbox “reset” that laid off thousands of employees and spun off four studios, Xbox CEO Asha Sharma wants to get Xbox back to growth. In a memo obtained by The Verge, Sharma told staff by the end of fiscal year 2027 (which runs through next June), “we will return XBOX to player and revenue growth.” Xbox will also “improve profits back in line with industry averages,” Sharma said. “Every function and studio will own part of that outcome.”\nSince taking over Xbox in February, Sharma has made sweeping changes to attempt to turn things around. Previously, she has cited challenges like a low accountability margin, increasing component costs for hardware, an “over extended” slate of studios, and platform infrastructure that’s “not built for the battle ahead.” In addition to the major structural changes, Xbox made Gears of War: E-Day and Clockwork Revolution Xbox console exclusives and is planning to do more. Sharma has also set an ambitious and nearly impossible goal to make Xbox “one of the few companies that entertains more than a billion people each day.”&nbsp;\nIn Thursday’s memo, Sharma laid out four Xbox priorities for fiscal year 2027:\n> CORE: Strengthen our platform, led by console&nbsp;&nbsp;\n> CONTENT: Grow great games into global franchises&nbsp;&nbsp;\n> CREATION: Make Minecraft the world’s creator platform&nbsp;&nbsp;\n> CONNECTION: Extend the worlds that fans love\nXbox will measure progress toward its goals in three “stages,” Sharma said. The first is to return to growth by the end of fiscal year 2027. The second is to turn its bets into growth; “In FY28 and FY29, our Four C’s and roadmap must move into businesses producing meaningful player value and revenue acceleration.” The third is to “scale what works,” and by fiscal year 2030, “our ambition is to be halfway to our long-term daily-player goal with sustained double-digit growth in players and engagement and industry leading margins.”\nSharma shared some vague numbers about Xbox’s current playerbase. “XBOX is one of the largest stadiums in the world, bringing together more than 100 million people every day, more than 500 million each month, and nearly one billion each year,” she wrote. She also noted that console “generates the majority of XBOX revenue and remains the foundation of our fandom” and that it is still Xbox’s “flagship experience.” Xbox’s OS, Game Pass, Windows, and streaming “become our platform for new players and developers.”&nbsp;\nThe company is also shifting its studio system from a decentralized model to one that’s “more focused around our strongest franchises and biggest new ideas,” Sharma said — something that’s already become evident with changes like the big new Fallout push. Xbox will build “long-term plans” for its biggest franchises “across film, television, consumer products, sponsorship, live experiences, and form new partnerships globally, including China.” Three Xbox franchises “already generate more than $1 billion annually,” though Sharma doesn’t specify which those are.\nTo grow its share in casual games, Xbox plans to lean on Candy Crush-maker King as well as Microsoft Casual Games, Sharma said. And Xbox is planning to invest in Minecraft “more than ever before.”",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Entertainment",
        "Gaming",
        "Microsoft",
        "News",
        "Tech",
        "Xbox"
      ],
      "author": "Jay Peters",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-30T22:10:13.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/02/268364_Inside_Microsofts_big_Xbox_leadership_shakeup_CVirginia.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/games/973520/xbox-ceo-memo-one-year-growth",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
