    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-27-second-again-why-has-scheffler-stopped-winning",
      "title": "Second again - why has Scheffler stopped winning?",
      "summary": "World number one Scottie Scheffler has not won a tournament since January, but what's the reason?",
      "body": "Leaving it too late?At the top of his powers, Scheffler, 30, is a front-running machine.Of his 20 victories on tour, 75% have come when he has held the lead going into the final round, albeit his most recent three victories have been when trailing after 54 holes.This season however, he has not even been joint leader after the third round and therefore always been chasing on a Sunday.Take the 3M Open this weekend. He shot an eight-under 63 to finish second behind exciting new talent Koivun, but was still three shots back.Twice in 2026 he has lost in a play-off, to Matt Fitzpatrick and Viktor Hovland. Both times Scheffler trailed going into round four and duly reeled in the leader, only to fall short on the extra hole.Early in his career, Scheffler coughed up the lead after 54 holes a number of times, including when leading Rory McIlroy by six shots at the Tour Championship in 2022.Since then, when in front with 18 to play, he has ruthlessly converted. This season, he has not given himself that chance.Failing to put everything together at once?At his best, there is an inevitability to Scheffler winning - but over the course of any tournament, a dip in one element can cost him.For instance, The Open at Royal Birkdale was won brilliantly by New Zealand's Ryan Fox but, if Scheffler's putter had worked during the week, he would have reclaimed the title he took at Royal Portrush last year.In his second round alone, he missed seven birdie putts from 15 feet or less - four inside 10 feet - and was ranked 60th in the field on the greens.It is an Achilles' heel that has dogged Scheffler before, but the PGA Tour has him as fifth-best putter, external on tour in 2026 - so it is certainly not a problem every week.Or perhaps slow starts are costing him. On nine occasions this season, including at the Masters and the US Open, he has begun with a round of 70 or over, leaving himself playing catch-up for the rest of the tournament.A glance at Scheffler's record shows metronomic consistency across all facets of his game.Winning a golf tournament is just really, really hard to do.If wins are the only currency, both Matt Fitzpatrick and Cameron Young have three on tour this season, while Wyndham Clark has a regular victory and a US Open.But a glance at the FedEx Cup's season-long rankings reassert Scheffler's dominance.Players are awarded points for where they finish in a tournament - and Scheffler has 795 more than Fitzpatrick in second.That is because even though he has not win since January, he has done everything but.Sunday's second place at the 3M Open was his fifth runners-up finish - only the second golfer in 40 years to have at least five second places in a season on the PGA Tour.He has also finished third on two occasions, and in the top five 11 times across 17 starts in 2026. It is a body of work that dwarfs anything put together by his rivals.\"I think I've been really close to winning some tournaments and that can be frustrating,\" Scheffler said before the Scottish Open.\"At the same time, I've had some good results - a fourth and a second are not bad results by any means. Margins in golf are really small and I just keep trying to do my best.\"The lack of wins has not prevented him stretching his lead at the top of the Official World Golf rankings, external either.In fact, the distance between him and second-placed Rory McIlroy is equivalent to between McIlroy and America's Matt McCarty (in 67th).Scheffler has been world number one since 21 May 2023 - nearly 166 weeks. Woods' unbroken record stands at 281 weeks, between June 2005 and October 2010.That intimidating record is still more than two years off for Scheffler, but current evidence suggests knocking him off top spot will be extraordinarily difficult.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-27T13:06:46.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/bcd5/live/472f6700-89b9-11f1-ba35-adcc7c70146e.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/golf/articles/cm2ge4pv94lo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-07-27-should-you-wash-your-solar-panels",
      "title": "Should you wash your solar panels?",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://incoherency.co.uk/blog/stories/should-you-wash-your-solar-panels.html\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49069132\nPoints: 177\n# Comments: 154",
      "body": "I have a small solar farm and the panels have got visibly dusty. Is cleaning them worthwhile? How much difference does it make? Let's find out.\nI know that my panels have not been cleaned in the last year. I expect they also weren't cleaned in the year prior to that (why would you clean\nthem when you're about to sell the house?). But beyond that I don't know when they were last cleaned.\nThe short answer is that I think I got a 2%-5% increase in power output from my solar farm due to cleaning the panels, which will work out to\nabout £60-£150/yr, decaying to 0 over the course of a few years. So, probably just about worthwhile.\nThere are 16 panels in total, connected up to the inverter as 2 banks of 8 panels each. The inverter reports the power output from each\nbank individually, so the plan is to take a bunch of readings before starting, then wash all of the panels in one bank, taking readings\nin between and at the end.\nOur hypothesis is that cleaning the panels will increase power output.\nWe can test whether washing the panels has made any difference by looking at the ratio of power output from the 2 banks.\nIf we just looked at raw power output then it would be confounded by changing cloud cover, sun angle, etc.\nThere is still the fact that the 2 banks of panels are physically separate and plausibly one bank is better positioned for sun 45 minutes\nlater than the other. Ideally I would have been measuring the ratio of power output for several days prior to see how it varies throughout the day.\nThis is how the first row of panels looks after I've washed 3 of them, you can see the furthest one is noticeably grubbier:\nSo they were \"visibly dusty\", but not massively dirty. If your panels are dirtier than mine were, then your benefit from cleaning them\nwill be greater than mine was.\nMy results for cleaning one bank of panels are shown in this chart:\nWe see that the initial power ratio is very stable before the panels are washed.\nWe then step up to having washed \"half\" a panel (I initially tried to wash them with window cleaner and a paper towel, but this was ineffective\nso I then walked away to get a bucket of soapy water and a cloth, and then took a reading which I labelled as 50% washed).\nFor some reason the power ratio drops significantly when the first panel is washed, I'm unsure why.\nAnd then the power ratio increases as more panels are washed as we'd expect.\nBut once all the panels are washed, the power ratio drops off again while nothing changes. I am unsure whether this is because as the surface water evaporates off\nthe panels get slightly opaque again? Like the \"frosted glass effect\", where you can see through frosted glass when it is wet but it gets opaque\nagain when dry. Maybe beyond cleaning the panels I ought to be polishing them?\nAnyway it looks like cleaning the panels was about a 2%-5% improvement, depending on what you think is going on at the end.\nI got a bit of a tingle when I was cleaning one of the panels. At first I thought I was getting an electric shock from the wet panel, but\nI inspected my finger and found a tiny thistle splinter in it. After I removed the splinter it seemed fine.\nBut a bit later I got another\ntingle from another panel! There definitely wasn't a splinter in my finger any more, but the tingle was in the same place. I think the tingle\nactually was coming from the electricity, but I was only able to feel it at the point where the thistle had already\nChatGPT convinced me that there could just be a tiny \"capacitive leakage\" from an \"inverter with no transformer\", so I'm not going to worry\nabout it. But if I clean the panels again I will wait until dark lol.\nThe next question is should I be upgrading the solar farm?\nI think mine was installed about 15 years ago, and generates (at peak output) 3.7 kW from\nCorrect me if I'm wrong on any of this:\nReplacing the panels with more modern ones would increase the power output by about 60%, at a cost of about £5000, which would pay for itself in about 3\nyears, which seems like a no-brainer.\nHowever, due to the fact that my solar farm was installed so long ago, it benefits from a feed-in tariff, which means that not only do I get paid\nan absurdly high rate, but it is paid also based on the electricity I generate rather than what I export.\nIf I increase the power output\nof the system then the additional capacity will not be eligible for the feed-in tariff and will revert to present-day prevailing tariff which is about 4x worse before\nyou even consider that I currently get to use electricity and still get paid for generating it.\nThis is the yin and yang of market-distorting incentives. Today's incentive to install solar becomes tomorrow's disincentive to upgrading it.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "surprisetalk",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-27T13:04:11.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://img.incoherency.co.uk/6637/thumb",
      "originalUrl": "https://incoherency.co.uk/blog/stories/should-you-wash-your-solar-panels.html",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
