      "id": "theverge-2026-07-27-nvidia-microsoft-launch-open-ai-security-alliance-without-op",
      "title": "Nvidia, Microsoft launch open AI security alliance — without OpenAI, Google, or Anthropic",
      "summary": "Nvidia on Monday said it is joining forces with Microsoft, SpaceX, IBM, and other tech companies to build and share open-source AI security tools. \nThe new Open Secure AI Alliance said open tools are required to effectively defend against attacks from frontier models. The initiative is a direct response to mounting concerns over the safety of advanced AI systems after a rogue OpenAI model escaped ",
      "body": "Nvidia on Monday said it is joining forces with Microsoft, SpaceX, IBM, and other tech companies to build and share open-source AI security tools.&nbsp;\nThe new Open Secure AI Alliance said open tools are required to effectively defend against attacks from frontier models. The initiative is a direct response to mounting concerns over the safety of advanced AI systems after a rogue OpenAI model escaped containment and attacked another company during testing. That company, Hugging Face, said it was forced to use a Chinese open-weight model to defend itself due to the strict safety guardrails limiting the usefulness of top US models.\nFounding members include Palantir, OpenClaw, the Linux Foundation, Cloudflare, Cloudera, Dell, Cisco, Adobe, Siemens, and DoorDash. Conspicuously absent are leading US AI companies, including OpenAI, Google, and Anthropic.&nbsp;\nThe alliance arrives amid growing tensions over whether the world’s most capable AI models should remain open. Chinese companies have released increasingly powerful open-weight models, notably Moonshot AI’s Kimi K3, challenging the strategy pursued by US labs that have largely kept frontier systems closed and proprietary. Nvidia and its partners argue that securing AI requires access to both closed and open models, stressing that defenders need the tools to counter emerging threats.&nbsp;\nThe announcement also follows reports that the Trump administration considered restricting access to cutting-edge Chinese models and an industry movement — again spearheaded by Nvidia — defending the need for openness in AI. Google and OpenAI signed that letter belatedly, too, though Anthropic remains absent.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "News",
        "Nvidia",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Robert Hart",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-27T12:06:22.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/STKP210_JENSEN_HUANG_A.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/971281/nvidia-open-secure-ai-alliance-cybersecurity",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-27-nans-who-befriended-pro-wrestler-on-flight-to-vegas-in-talks",
      "title": "Nans who befriended pro wrestler on flight to Vegas in talks to make a film",
      "summary": "Four grandmothers who befriended a pro wrestler are in talks about bringing their story to the big screen.",
      "body": "What began as a chance encounter on a flight to Las Vegas could soon be heading to the big screen, as a group of grandmothers and a pro-wrestler discuss a film based on their story.Lynn Crawford, 71, Susie Matthews, 73, Rose Sulley, 74, and Christine Goodwin, 80, met DeReiss Gordon - known in the ring as Man Like DeReiss - on the flight in April.They were travelling to the city to scatter the ashes of Rose's late husband, Pete. Upon boarding the flight, there was confusion - with Gordon in one of the seats they mistakenly believed they had booked - before a friendship was formed, and the nans   took Gordon up on his offer to watch him at WrestleMania.Film producer Nick Barton said he believes this \"extraordinary story could make a really brilliant and successful movie\".The story captured hearts as well as headlines, including producer of Calendar Girls and the Full Monty Nick Barton, who has \"approached the group of friends\" about the remarkable story that \"wasn't meant to happen\" being turned into a film.\"When I first heard about this story in May, it reminded me of Calendar Girls and Kinky Boots, which Harbour Pictures and I produced for Disney and Buena Vista. \"I immediately contacted DeReiss and the Welsh nanas. \"I believe this extraordinary story could make a really brilliant and successful movie and am now working on the next steps towards its production.\"Discussing the possibility of their unlikely friendship being turned into a film, Crawford said: \"When we'd first met, we'd laughed that this should be a movie.\"Four nanas and a wrestler! It'd be brilliant.\"\"I couldn't believe it at first,\" said Gordon. \"Then I checked it out and was like, 'WOW! A movie'. This could happen. There's a way to go but we believe this would be a great movie.\"Speaking to BBC Radio Wales Breakfast, Sulley described the experience as being \"like a dream\" which the four nans never want to \"wake up from\".On Sunday, the group reunited to take part in a wrestling showcase in Cardiff, where they were trained by professional wrestlers before entering the ring themselves. People were holding banners with their photos chanting \"Nanas! Nanas!\".This unlikely friendship has created something of a fan base in the wrestling world.\"It was absolutely hilarious\", Sulley said. The four women admitted they knew \"nothing\" about wrestling before meeting Gordon earlier this year.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-27T12:04:15.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/da51/live/9dedc2d0-89a5-11f1-8a9d-b9e590fcf96d.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/czxq4zl5jp2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-27-meta-is-royally-screwing-up-its-smart-glasses-rollout",
      "title": "Meta is royally screwing up its smart glasses rollout",
      "summary": "We’re leaning in with the playing card metaphor.\t\n\nAcross New York City, London, and Washington, DC, ads for Meta's smart glasses have been plastered over with satirical posters from activist groups. One guerrilla ad calls these \"the biggest advancement in pervert technology since the trenchcoat.\" Another transposes the words \"mass surveillance predator glasses\" over influencer Kylie Jenner's face",
      "body": "Across New York City, London, and Washington, DC, ads for Meta’s smart glasses have been plastered over with satirical posters from activist groups. One guerrilla ad calls these “the biggest advancement in pervert technology since the trenchcoat.” Another transposes the words “mass surveillance predator glasses” over influencer Kylie Jenner’s face, calling her out for partnering with the company as a glasses ambassador.&nbsp;&nbsp;\nThe message of these ads is clear. These glasses may violate your privacy — and would you trust Meta?\nMeta seems spooked. For so long, the company’s privacy efforts for smart glasses have essentially relied on a single LED light and a policy that could be summed up as “don’t be a jerk.” Unfortunately, plenty of Meta glassholes have emerged over the last three years, filming often-sexualized videos of others without their consent, sometimes with the LED disabled. In a recent conversation with The Verge, Meta’s VP of wearables, Alex Himel, acknowledged that the company has become aware that an increasing number of people have tampered with the devices.&nbsp;\nThis explains why the company recently announced a mandatory software update that will disable the camera on all its glasses if the privacy light is physically tampered with. Last week, Instagram head Adam Mosseri said that the social media platform will take down videos taken with Meta’s glasses that depict harassment. Instagram might have opened a can of worms concerning content moderation, but attempts to curtail misuse are good. The problem is Meta could have been doing this all along.&nbsp;\n[Image: Activist groups have plastered satirical anti-Meta Ray-Ban ads across New York City, Washington D.C., and London. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/268667_meta_glasses_spying_posters_AKrales_0059.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nWe can’t talk about the current era of smart glasses without acknowledging that Meta has made a series of savvy decisions. Where Google Glass was loud, the Ray-Ban Meta glasses were normal and discreet. Bose beat Meta to the idea of audio glasses by a few years, and Snap was first to the idea of glasses recording POV content for social platforms. But Meta was the one that executed on making affordable, name-brand, and genuinely stylish glasses by partnering with EssilorLuxottica. It’s a model that Google and Samsung are now emulating by partnering with Warby Parker and Gentle Monster. Other companies may have been first to the concept of smart glasses, but Meta notably has been the closest to making these genuinely mainstream.\nExcept, in recent months, Meta’s been playing its cards all wrong. First there was the NYT report that Meta was looking to roll out facial recognition tech for the glasses while privacy advocates were distracted. Then there was the Wired report that said Meta had silently shipped — but not activated — that tech to existing glasses. Now, there’s a Financial Times report that alleges Meta wants these glasses to record 24/7. Outside these larger beats, Meta has done things like remove the ability to opt out of voice recordings training its AI. Its executives occasionally say tone-deaf things like those who eschew smart glasses will be at a “cognitive disadvantage.” And amid legitimate privacy concerns following bad actors misusing the tech, the company has seeded these glasses to content creators alongside a splashy campaign with Kylie Jenner — one of the most visible but polarizing celebrities.&nbsp;\nIn a vacuum, a gaffe or two happens. When you screw up this many times, it starts to look like you’re purposefully missing the point.\n[Image: Focusing on the privacy LED light, experts say, isn’t enough on its own. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/268639_Meta_Adventurer_glasses_AKrales_0005.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nAnanthakrishnan is skeptical that smart glasses will take off outside specific workplaces, not just because of social stigma, but because societal attitudes toward Big Tech companies and what’s shared online have shifted. Case in point, Meta’s executives have often pointed to the early days of the smartphone in defense of smart glasses. Back then, the public was unsure about these devices, but eventually, everyone figured out what the acceptable social norms ought to be. Ananthakrishnan disagrees.\n“Tech companies are not at all looked upon with favor right now. Compounded with the fact that we live in an age of social media, where anything that we say and everything that we do — we could be liable for it. There’s a lot more awareness that there are bad people on the internet who could do really terrible things compared to the smartphone era,” says Ananthakrishnan. She also points out how people feel about individual privacy is different than when you are responsible for others’ privacy.\nTake smartwatches. Ananthakrishnan notes that people are willing to risk their own health data privacy because there’s a clear benefit to their goals. However, if there are negative outcomes — for example, your health data getting leaked to your employer — you’re the only one who suffers, and you’re the only victim. With smart glasses, the privacy calculus changes. You’re the person risking others’ privacy, possibly without consent — the perpetrator.\n[Image: Wearing smart glasses forces you to wonder: who else’s privacy am I putting at risk? https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/268639_Meta_Adventurer_glasses_AKrales_0222.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nSo is it even possible for Meta — or any other smart glasses company — to reassure a skittish public? Or are its recent attempts simply too little, too late?\n“There’s not much Meta can do to convince me they genuinely care about user privacy when their whole business model is built on invading it,” says Kendall Schrohe, executive director of the Attention Sphere, one of the advocacy groups affiliated with the anti-Meta glasses ads in DC and New York City. “At the end of the day, there is nothing privacy-preserving about sunglasses with a built-in camera. This is surveillance technology and no amount of tweaking changes that.”\nSchrohe says that Meta’s credibility is further undermined by the fact that it, along with other tech companies, reportedly lobbied to weaken state privacy bills.&nbsp;\n[Image: The societal stigma, plus fatigue with Big Tech, is one reason for the backlash against smart glasses. Meta’s reputation is another. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/268667_meta_glasses_spying_posters_AKrales_0142.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nBarring a dramatic overhaul, it’s going to be hard for Meta and Big Tech at large to change the current narrative. And it’s tempting to think that, perhaps, some tech execs might throw up their hands and give up on pursuing proactive privacy at all. To lean into the surveillance dystopia until external actors, like legislators and private businesses, force their privacy hand.&nbsp;\nBut the reality is that we’re all watching to see what card Meta — the current leader in this space — plays next. Continuing to run the same strategy all but ensures the company will either hand the mantle to Google or Apple, or tank smart glasses as a whole. If Meta wants even the smallest chance of succeeding, it simply has to do something different.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Analysis",
        "Gadgets",
        "Meta",
        "Privacy",
        "Report",
        "Tech",
        "Wearable"
      ],
      "author": "Victoria Song",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-27T12:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/268639_Meta_Adventurer_glasses_AKrales_0109.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/970948/meta-smart-glasses-privacy-wearables",
      "readTimeMinutes": 6,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-27-uk-s-iconic-jodrell-bank-telescope-faces-axe-in-science-cuts",
      "title": "UK's iconic Jodrell Bank telescope faces axe in science cuts",
      "summary": "Astronomers warn it would tear the heart out of UK science, and leave a generation untrained.",
      "body": "The Jodrell Bank Observatory, which has housed the UK's largest telescope for nearly 70 years, has lost its funding and could now close, the BBC understands.The observatory and its network of telescopes have been pivotal to the country's study of distant galaxies and training future astronomers. Scientists have described the decision as \"heartbreaking\" and \"scientifically misguided\".All scientific observations at Jodrell are expected to cease on the 1 April 2028 unless alternative funding can be found.The funding body, the UK Research and Innovation (UKRI) agency, said it had to focus investment on projects that deliver \"the greatest long-term impact\".Prof Brian Cox, professor of particle physics at the University of Manchester - which hosts Jodrell Bank Observatory - described the cuts as \"hasty and ill-considered\".  \"The damage to the UK's science base has already been substantial. I am astonished that UKRI are still ploughing ahead and not working with the Burnham government to find a solution,\" he said.In response, Jonathan Reynolds, who is the new Secretary of State in the expanded business department that now has responsibility for science, told BBC News that the UK's world class research was \"absolutely central to creating a better future for the UK\".\"I am committed to working with UKRI to ensure the UK remains able not only to sustain its world-leading research capabilities, but also to invest in the next generation of scientific facilities and infrastructure that will underpin discovery, innovation and growth in the years ahead.\"The Jodrell Bank site was first established in 1945 and pioneered radio astronomy and the study of galaxies beyond our own. It was also pivotal during the Cold War, when it was used to monitor Soviet missiles during the height of nuclear tensions between the USSR and Nato powers. Its contribution to global scientific efforts earned it Unesco World Heritage status in 2019. But the UKRI, which has an annual budget of just under £10bn, says it has had to cut the observatory's annual £2.8m budget because planned research costs for physics and the running of its labs had escalated.The announcement is part of broader cuts to astronomy, nuclear and particle physics research over the next four years which will also impact on other projects. The UK will no longer participate in research at the James Clark Maxwell Telescope in Hawaii, which scientists are using to image supermassive black holes at the centre of galaxies. UKRI has also cut funding for the UK's involvement in two instruments on the Extremely Large Telescope which is being built in Chile to search for signs of life on other Earth-like worlds.And a number of upgrades to improve experiments at the Large Hadron Collider will not be paid for by the agency.First celestial image unveiled from revolutionary telescope in ChileTwenty-eight jobs are at risk at the Jodrell Bank site in Cheshire, but its closure would also affect the work of nearly 3,000 scientists who use it in their research.Prof Simon Garrington, associate director of the Jodrell Bank Observatory, told BBC News that everyone at Jodrell Bank was \"devastated\" by the decision.\"It's not just because of the fantastic science that we do, but Jodrell Bank and its telescope network [have been] a national capability for nearly 70 years and a beacon for science in the north-west [of England], attracting young people into science and engineering careers,\" he said.The importance of the site to science and the community has seen it immortalised in popular science fiction works from the Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy to Doctor Who. Prof Garrington said the UKRI's decision would be challenged, but added that if those discussions failed the leadership team would do all it could to find alternative funding to keep the iconic Lovell Telescope, which is at the heart of the observatory and the network of telescopes it supports, continuing in some form.\"It is a huge hill to climb to try to establish a new way to fund this if UKRI will not fund any of it,\" he said.Scotland's Astronomer Royal, Prof Catherine Heymans, who was speaking for the UK's community of astronomers, described the decision by UKRI as \"a real act of national self-harm\".Despite its age, continuous upgrades have kept the Lovell telescope at the cutting edge of astronomical research. It is the third largest steerable radio telescope in the world, and its science was supercharged when it was connected to six other telescopes across central England, to create the e-Merlin network. Joined together these UK telescopes can zoom into objects in deep space and see them in incredible detail - with similar resolution to the Hubble space telescope.The e-Merlin network is being used for research into:How planets and their parent stars form.How galaxies evolve, and the role played by supermassive black holes.The invisible dark matter and dark energy that make up more than 95% of the Universe.The e-Merlin network is part of a pan-European network of telescopes - without it, images of distant stars and galaxies will be less clear. Prof Michael Kramer, is executive director of the Max Planck Institute for Radio Astronomy in Bonn, and a highly respected scientist at the University of Manchester. He told BBC News that he believed the decision by UKRI to be \"scientifically, technologically as well as economically short-sighted\".But another important role of Jodrell Bank Observatory is training UK astronomers in the science of radio astronomy. Astronomers have claimed that the closure of Lovell and the e-Merlin network will mean that Britain will fall behind in a field that was pioneered at Jodrell Bank.\"Internationally, people will look at that and will hardly believe it. You're cutting off the radio astronomy capability in the country,\" Prof Kramer said. The cut comes at a time when other countries are investing in their own national observatories to prepare for, what many astronomers believe will be, the dawn of a golden age in radio astronomy. This is because of the construction of a new super radio telescope in South Africa and Australia called the Square Kilometre Array (SKA).",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
